WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.509 --> 00:00:18.599
Here at VisualPolitik we talked about how
we now live in a world where every day there

00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:21.640
is less poverty and people are able to live
better lives.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:27.480
A change that is even reaching the most unprotected
region in the world, sub-Saharan Africa.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:33.620
We already talked about the African lions,
and paid special attention to cases like Rwanda.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:37.800
However, we still need to talk about when
and why this region of the world, which so

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.989
many considered hopelessly destitute, began
to change.

00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:49.499
Well, that is precisely what we are going
to talk about in this video--that and what

00:00:49.499 --> 00:00:59.829
we can expect in the upcoming years.

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:04.400
For years talking about Africa has also meant
talking about extreme poverty, natural disasters

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.000
and all kinds of conflicts.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.250
And in a way, things are still that way.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:12.930
See, no more or less than half, yes, half
of all the people who suffer from extreme

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.810
poverty in the world live in the sub-Saharan
African region.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.810
We’re talking about almost 400 million people.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.000
Also, news like this isn’t surprising either:

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.230
“We are facing the largest humanitarian
crisis since the creation of the United Nations

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:30.430
[in 1945].

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:36.000
(...) Now, more than 20 million people across
four countries face starvation and famine”.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.380
- Stephen O’Brien, the UN under secretary-general
for humanitarian affairs

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.370
Due to all of this, I’m sure nobody is a
stranger to those NGO campaigns seeking to

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.840
raise funds for all kinds of humanitarian
catastrophes in Africa.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.320
Or for governments to allocate billions of
dollars each year for Official Development

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.320
Assistance.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.620
In fact, government aid is as high as it’s
ever been: every year, the African continent

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.090
receives about 50 billion dollars.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:04.630
And this has been going on for... decades.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:09.780
However, the truth is that for years all this
money didn’t help that much.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:22.530
Let's take a look at, for example, the 90s.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:26.510
While international aid in this area of the
world amounted to tens and tens of billions...

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.230
What do you think happened with poverty?

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.379
Do you think it decreased?

00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:31.959
Well... no, none of that.

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:41.400
Between 1990 and 1998, poverty levels went
from 55% to 58%.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.359
But how could that be?

00:02:44.359 --> 00:02:47.219
Well… because intentions are one thing.

00:02:47.219 --> 00:02:49.129
Reality is a very different one….

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:54.769
Some experts claim that for years official
aid weakened local producers.

00:02:54.769 --> 00:03:00.820
“Evidence overwhelmingly demonstrates that
aid to Africa has made the poor poorer, and

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:01.969
the growth slower.”

00:03:01.969 --> 00:03:07.299
Dambisa Moyo an economist and author of "Dead
Aid: Why Aid Is Not Working and How There

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:08.950
Is a Better Way for Africa."

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:13.079
For example, in Tanzania it was cheaper to
buy subsidized milk from the European Union

00:03:13.079 --> 00:03:14.430
than from local producers

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:18.239
However, my friends, with the beginning of
the new century, suddenly everything began

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:19.239
to change …

00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:28.500
THE GREAT CHANGE

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:34.999
Suddenly, when Africa seemed helpless, everything
began to change.

00:03:34.999 --> 00:03:39.079
With the beginning of the 21st century, statistics
turned around...

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:43.620
Since the year 2000, extreme poverty has dropped
by almost 40%; average life expectancy has

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.590
grown by more
than 10 years; infant mortality has decreased

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:51.919
to less than half; the same happened with
HIV infections in children and virtually any

00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:53.110
other statistic.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:04:02.120
If we look at the economy, we’ll be able
to see how between the year 2000 and the year

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.099
2015 sub-Saharan Africa economies, excluding
South Africa – which is a bit of a peculiar

00:04:06.099 --> 00:04:11.439
case, as we saw here in VisualPolitik – grew
an average by 6%, which is more than twice

00:04:11.439 --> 00:04:13.569
than, for example, Latin America.

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:17.459
Another great indicator of how things are
changing is tourism.

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:22.030
Yes, tourism, you heard it.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:26.669
The tourism sector has some peculiar characteristics
that reveal a lot of information.

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:31.370
On the one hand, this sector has great transformational
power for the poorest because it’s very

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:35.590
labor-intensive; and on the other hand, we
need to take into account that people don’t

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:41.620
vacation in countries that are at war, or
have famine or epidemics.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.910
When this industry improves in the poorest
countries, it is usually a good indicator

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.940
that things are improving as a whole.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:52.700
Well, if we see the date, we might be quite
surprised, because this sector, my friends,

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:58.000
has been growing at an average annual rate,
of no less than 9% for 20 years.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.790
That is two full decades.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:07.260
Yes, it seems like things are moving forward.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:13.820
But at this point, you may be wondering something.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.400
I certainly am.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:19.350
What on earth happened to lead to such a change?

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:24.540
Check out this graph, which may give us a
clue:

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:30.900
But what exactly led to this kind of "miracle"?

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.660
Let's see.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.560
PREPARING FOR TAKE-OFF

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:42.230
Many things drive change, but we will now
see how three particular events in Africa

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:46.020
began to change, and I think forever, this
continent’s reality.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.580
The first one was remittances.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.810
See, in Africa the population grows very quickly,
which along with the terrible living conditions,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:57.580
has led many Africans to emigrate to richer
countries in the past few decades.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.610
Today, these people send tens of billions
of dollars home every year.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.890
This money is used in local markets or to
set up businesses, for example.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:09.580
These are remittances, which are actually
much more important than development aid.

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:10.580
Can immigration be a good solidarity policy?

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:11.580
Well... it looks like it may be.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:12.580
Second, and very importantly.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:17.090
In March 2000, the US Congress finally approved
a law that had been promoted by Bill Clinton

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:21.390
in his last years in command: the "African
Growth and Opportunity act".

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:25.500
This is basically a law that establishes a
preferential trade agreement that eliminates

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:31.330
tariffs and reduces barriers to trade thousands
and thousands of products from sub-Saharan

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.000
Africa.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.740
See, since this agreement was approved, we
can see that between 2000 and 2016 – which

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:44.220
wasn’t exactly a good year – trade between
the United States and sub-Saharan Africa multiplied

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:52.290
by 2.5, which means a 6% growth rate every
year.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:53.930
And third, perhaps the key to everything:
China.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:55.900
Yes, you heard it right.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:01.030
In 2001 China joined the World Trade Organization
and became something like "the factory of

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:02.030
the world".

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:05.300
Well, what has happened since then has been
quite impressive: China's huge growth has

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:16.180
lifted tens of millions of people out of poverty...
both in China and in Africa.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:33.530
Allow me to explain myself, the Asian giant’s
gigantic growth has increased this country’s

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:37.810
demand for all kinds of products: raw materials,
food, manufacturing...

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:38.890
everything!

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:42.500
Well, many African countries took advantage
of this opportunity.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:48.280
Since the year 2000, trade between China and
Africa has multiplied by no less than 17 times,

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:51.330
representing an annual growth rate of almost
20%.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:56.170
Since 2009, China is already, by far, the
continent’s largest trading partner.

00:07:56.170 --> 00:08:03.430
But not only that, the Chinese, as we mentioned
here, have also been investing a lot of money

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:04.620
all over the world.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:08.070
And Africa is no exception.

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:15.410
In 2016, 4 out of 10 euros that foreigners
invested in Africa came from China.

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:19.310
Today more than 10,000 Chinese companies are
doing business in the African continent, mostly

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:22.390
private companies that work in all kinds of
industries.

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:26.000
And that number just keeps rising.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:32.490
Yes, my friends, this is how Africa has managed
to start turning the statistics around.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:36.130
The great catalyst for this entire change
wasn’t some kind of super-earthly miracle

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.690
nor did it have much to do with development
aid.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:41.899
This change was driven by trade and investment.

00:08:41.899 --> 00:08:44.500
And, Africans themselves seem to see it very
clearly.

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:45.550
Don’t believe me?

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:46.970
Well... take a look:

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:52.440
“African countries are forging ahead to
complete negotiations for a continental free

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:56.690
trade area between 55 countries by early next
year.

00:08:56.690 --> 00:09:01.940
The idea, adopted by the African Union in
2012, is to create a single market which includes

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:05.029
the free movement of goods, services and people.”

00:09:05.029 --> 00:09:09.880
Yes, that's right, Africa is working to expand
trade to every corner of the continent.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:14.170
Not all countries are developing equally,
but this quite normal in all parts of the

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.870
world.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.340
FOLLOWING THE ASIAN STAR

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:25.130
At this point, we need to ask ourselves another
question.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.040
Can we really dream of Africa's final takeoff?

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:33.650
See, I suspect that many of you may be thinking:
well everything you say in VisualPolitik is

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:36.010
very nice... but it isn’t a general rule.

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:40.360
Surely, in Africa there are countries that
are doing well and others that are doing not

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.360
so well.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:42.360
Well, you know what?

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:43.450
That's true, but it's also... normal.

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:48.320
See, it may sound shocking today, but in the
50s, Asia was as poor as Africa.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:49.320
You heard that right.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.790
Yes, I know, seeing cities like Shanghai,
Hong Kong or Taipei makes it very hard to

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.860
imagine that they were once so poor.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:59.620
But, when South Korea or Taiwan began to grow,
they weren’t richer than Ghana or Senegal,

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:00.620
for example.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:05.880
And China, India or Vietnam were, of course,
much poorer.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:09.000
How things have changed!

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.720
Well, we can get some ideas from this:

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:17.710
My friends, Asian countries didn’t grow
all at once either, not at all.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:22.839
While Korea, Singapore, Japan and Taiwan grew,
other countries such as China, Indonesia and

00:10:22.839 --> 00:10:25.650
India remained immersed in poverty.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:28.890
Also, they weren’t exactly democratic models.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:32.550
South Korea and Taiwan were under hard dictatorships
until very recently.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:35.270
Singapore is in a similar state... and China...

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:37.100
well what can I say...

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:44.840
In short, lots of poverty, some more advanced
countries with others further behind, dictatorships...

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.220
what we are seeing in Africa today isn’t
so different from what was happening in Asia

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:55.190
50 years ago.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.590
READY FOR TAKE-OFF?

00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:01.140
Yes, things in Africa are changing.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:07.670
Out of the 10 countries that are apparently
growing the most in 2017, 5 are here, in Africa.

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:14.960
If we take a look at the latest Doing Business,
the World Bank indicator for measuring how

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:19.280
easy it is to do business in certain economies,
we can see that out of the 10 countries that

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.770
have improved the most, 4 are also African.

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:28.200
True, poverty is still very high, and these
countries are facing a difficult challenge:

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:32.630
such large population growth that the continent’s
population will actually multiply in the upcoming

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:33.630
decades.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.680
However, my friends, the good news is that
everything indicates that thanks to trade

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:42.001
and international investment, Africa is finding
its way and is maybe, once and for all, ready

00:11:42.001 --> 00:11:43.001
for take-off.

00:11:43.001 --> 00:11:46.440
But now it’s your turn, do you think that
Africa will emulate the development that Asia

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.360
has had in recent decades?

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:52.130
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:56.610
I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did and don’t forget to

00:11:56.610 --> 00:12:00.160
subscribe to our channel for brand new videos
every Monday and Thursday.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.840
Also, don’t forget to check out our friends
at the Reconsider Media Podcast - they provided

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:06.149
the vocals in this episode that were not mine!

00:12:06.149 --> 00:12:07.649
And as always, thanks for watching!

